      "role": "user",
      "content": "we've done the cleaning and checked the oils, everything is spotless. the chemical itself seems fine too because it works on our test samples but fails on the main production run. it's just so weird how it happens sporadically. like, one batch is perfect and the next is totally washed out for no reason. could there be some kind of environmental factor or something subtle interfering with the transfer during the actual press cycle?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your situation could be linked to a few environmental or machine-related issues. If your heat press is not uniformly distributing heat or if there are fluctuations during running times, this could explain the inconsistency. Large temperature and pressure variances even within the same cycle can affect the ink transfer.\n\n\nConsider the following:\n\n- Calibrate your heat press to make sure it's distributing heat evenly across the surface.\n\n- Investigate the stability of your power supply. Large voltage differences can affect the evenness of the heat press.\n\n- Look at the production environment. Humidity and ambient temperature can impact the heat press's operation.\n\n- Test your system after a maintenance check-up. Ensure consistency throughout all settings.\n\n- Examine if there's a variation in paper or substrate within the batch that could explain the inconsistency.\n\n- Lastly, investigate if anything in the production line, such as fumes or particles, could possibly affect the sublimation in a sporadic manner. It's crucial that all machinery and surroundings are maintained as sterile as possible for the sublimation process to be successful.",
